/* 166 条定义的英文译文。键是 DEF 的字典键（格名），与 fillDef 的查找方式一致。 */
window.MM_DEFS = {"定点":{"d":"A point is a 0-dimensional affine space without direction. In coordinates it is the unique solution of the system x₁=a₁, …, xₙ=aₙ — n linear equations, zero free variables.","t":"The codimension of a point equals the dimension of the ambient space: a point in Rⁿ is an affine subspace of codimension n."},"平面上的点":{"d":"A point in the plane is an element (x, y) of R². R² is at the same time a 2-dimensional real vector space and a 2-dimensional Euclidean space — length and angle exist only after the standard inner product is added.","t":"R² and R are equinumerous as sets (Cantor), but their dimensions differ: dimension is a notion of linear structure, not of cardinality."},"空间中的点":{"d":"A point in space is an element (x, y, z) of R³; three real numbers are needed to locate it. Adding the standard inner product gives 3-dimensional Euclidean space.","t":"A right-handed and a left-handed system in R³ cannot be transformed into each other by rotation, only by reflection — this is decided by the sign of det."},"四维点":{"d":"A four-dimensional point is an element (x, y, z, w) of R⁴. It cannot be drawn directly; the figure here is a two-stage projection: first a rotation in four dimensions, then projection to R³, then orthogonal projection to the plane.","t":"Projection preserves neither distance nor angle, but it preserves which points are connected — the combinatorial structure is invariant under projection."},"一元一次":{"d":"An equation of the form ax + b = 0 (a ≠ 0). Its solution set is the single point {−b/a}; geometrically it is the intersection of a 1-dimensional affine subspace with the coordinate axis.","t":"For a ≠ 0 the solution is unique; for a = 0 and b ≠ 0 there is no solution; for a = b = 0 the solution set is the whole space — the three cases exhaust all possibilities for rank and consistency."},"直线":{"d":"The set of points in the plane determined by a linear equation Ax + By + C = 0 (A and B not both zero), that is, a 1-dimensional affine subspace; the direction vector and the normal vector (A, B) are perpendicular.","t":"The relative position of two lines (intersecting, parallel, coincident) is determined entirely by the rank of the coefficient matrix, with no case distinction needed."},"平面":{"d":"The set of points in R³ determined by Ax + By + Cz + D = 0 (normal vector (A,B,C) ≠ 0); it is a 2-dimensional affine subspace. The normal vector determines the plane, and any two non-collinear vectors in the plane determine its direction.","t":"The distance from a point to a plane is |Ax₀+By₀+Cz₀+D| / √(A²+B²+C²); two planes are parallel iff their normal vectors are proportional."},"超平面":{"d":"The n−1 dimensional affine subspace of Rⁿ determined by a single linear equation a₁x₁ + ⋯ + aₙxₙ = b (coefficients not all zero), with normal vector a.","t":"A hyperplane divides Rⁿ into two half-spaces; the decision boundary of a linear classifier (perceptron, SVM) is a hyperplane, which is the geometric meaning of linear separability."},"一元二次":{"d":"ax² + bx + c = 0 (a ≠ 0). Over the complex numbers there are always two roots (counted with multiplicity); the number of real roots is decided by the sign of the discriminant Δ = b² − 4ac: Δ>0 two real roots, Δ=0 a double root, Δ<0 a conjugate pair of complex roots.","t":"Vieta's formulas: x₁ + x₂ = −b/a, x₁x₂ = c/a. The fundamental theorem of algebra guarantees that a polynomial of degree n over C has exactly n roots (counted with multiplicity), so a quadratic equation is always solvable over C."},"二次曲面":{"d":"The zero set in R³ of an equation F(x,y,z) = 0 of degree two in three unknowns. With the inertia index (p, q, r) of the quadratic part as a complete invariant, there are 17 normal forms under orthogonal transformations: 9 non-degenerate (ellipsoid, hyperboloid of one sheet, hyperboloid of two sheets, cone, elliptic paraboloid, hyperbolic paraboloid, elliptic cylinder, hyperbolic cylinder, parabolic cylinder) + 8 degenerate types (a point, a line, two intersecting lines, a plane, two parallel planes, the empty set, and so on).","t":"Sylvester's law of inertia: the normal form of a real quadratic form under invertible linear substitution is determined uniquely by the positive and negative inertia indices, so the classification of quadric surfaces is finite and exhaustive."},"二次型":{"d":"A quadratic form in n unknowns over a number field K is a homogeneous polynomial of degree two, Q(x) = xᵀAx, with A a symmetric matrix. Congruence A ↦ PᵀAP (P invertible) preserves the rank and, in the real case, the inertia index.","t":"Sylvester's law of inertia: the normal form of a real quadratic form under invertible linear substitution is determined uniquely by the positive and negative inertia indices (p, q), with p + q = rank A. Positive definiteness is equivalent to all leading principal minors being positive (Hurwitz criterion)."},"矩阵":{"d":"An m×n matrix is the coordinate representation of a linear map A: Kⁿ → Kᵐ with respect to given bases; matrix multiplication corresponds to composition of maps. The determinant det A is the signed volume scaling factor.","t":"Invertible ⇔ det A ≠ 0 ⇔ rank n ⇔ the null space contains only the zero vector. Similarity A ↦ P⁻¹AP does not change the characteristic polynomial."},"三次方程":{"d":"ax³ + bx² + cx + d = 0 (a ≠ 0). Over C there are always three roots (counted with multiplicity); the number of real roots is decided by the sign of the discriminant Δ = 18abcd − 4b³d + b²c² − 4ac³ − 27a²d².","t":"Cardano's formula gives a solution by radicals. But the Galois group of the general cubic is S₃, whose derived series contains A₃, so cube roots must appear in the solution — this is the root cause of why the cubic forces complex numbers into view (casus irreducibilis)."},"三次型":{"d":"A cubic form is F(x) = Σ_{i,j,k=1}^{n} t_{ijk}·x_i·x_j·x_k, where t is a symmetric tensor of order three; symmetry makes different orderings of the indices give the same monomial, so the number of independent coefficients is C(n+2, 3). For n = 3 its zero set is a plane cubic curve.","t":"A smooth cubic surface contains exactly 27 lines (Cayley–Salmon theorem), and the configuration of these 27 lines is invariant under birational equivalence; this is the starting point of enumerative algebraic geometry."},"椭圆曲线":{"d":"An elliptic curve over a field K is a nonsingular projective algebraic curve of genus 1 together with a K-rational point O. When char K ≠ 2, 3 it can be given by the short Weierstrass equation y² = x³ + ax + b, with nonsingularity condition 4a³ + 27b² ≠ 0. For K = C it is at the same time the complex manifold C/Λ (Λ a rank 2 lattice), that is, a compact Riemann surface of genus 1.","t":"Mordell–Weil theorem: E(K) is a finitely generated abelian group, E(K) ≅ Z^r ⊕ E(K)_tors. The integer r is called the rank; whether it can be arbitrarily large is still an open problem."},"椭圆曲线的 −P":{"d":"On y² = x³ + ax + b, the inverse −P of a point P is its reflection in the x-axis. Define P + Q as the inverse of the third intersection point of the line through P and Q with the curve; then O (the point at infinity) is the identity element.","t":"This operation makes E an abelian variety; geometrically, three points are collinear ⇔ P+Q+R = O, which compresses all the group axioms into a single collinearity condition."},"四次方程":{"d":"ax⁴ + bx³ + cx² + dx + e = 0. Ferrari's method reduces it to a product of two quadratic equations, so a quartic equation is always solvable by radicals.","t":"The Galois group of the general quartic is S₄, and S₄ is solvable — this is precisely the group-theoretic reason why a solution by radicals exists. Degree five is the boundary: S₅ is not solvable."},"五次及以上":{"d":"The general equation of degree n (n ≥ 5) has no solution by radicals. The reason is that its Galois group is Sₙ, and Sₙ is not solvable for n ≥ 5.","t":"Galois criterion: an equation is solvable by radicals ⇔ its Galois group is solvable. Note that the general equation having no universal formula does not mean that every quintic is unsolvable — x⁵ − 2 = 0 is obviously solvable."},"高次曲线":{"d":"A plane algebraic curve defined by an irreducible polynomial F(x, y) = 0 of degree d ≥ 5. Genus and singularities are related by the formula g = (d−1)(d−2)/2 − Σ δ_P.","t":"Higher degree allows more singularities, so the genus does not increase monotonically with the degree — this is the central phenomenon of resolution of singularities and of Riemann–Roch theory."},"双纽线":{"d":"The lemniscate of Bernoulli is the locus of points whose distances to two fixed points have a constant product: (x²+y²)² = 2a²(x² − y²), or r² = 2a²cos 2θ in polar coordinates. It is the graph of the inverse function of the elliptic integral ∫dx/√(1−x⁴).","t":"It is the critical member of the Cassini oval family at a = c: for a slightly larger than c there are two separate ovals, for a slightly smaller than c a single oval with a narrow waist. The family undergoes a topological change at the critical point."},"六次曲线":{"d":"A plane curve defined by an irreducible polynomial of degree six. Degree six is the degree matching the genus formula g = (d−1)(d−2)/2 = 10 for plane curves (smooth case).","t":"A smooth sextic curve has genus 10; the upper bound on the number of allowed singularities comes from the constraint that the genus be non-negative."},"幂级数":{"d":"A series of functions of the form Σ aₙ(x − x₀)ⁿ. A radius of convergence R exists (Cauchy–Hadamard: 1/R = limsup |aₙ|^{1/n}); the series converges absolutely for |x−x₀| < R and uniformly on every compact subset.","t":"Inside the circle of convergence the series may be differentiated and integrated term by term, and its sum is analytic; conversely every analytic function is locally a power series (Taylor expansion) — this is the watershed between complex and real analysis."},"级数反演":{"d":"Let f(x) = a₁x + a₂x² + ⋯ (a₁ ≠ 0). Then there is a unique formal power series g with f(g(x)) = x. The coefficients of g are given by the Lagrange inversion formula.","t":"Lagrange inversion: [xⁿ] g(x) = (1/n)[x^{n−1}] (x/f(x))ⁿ. It is a standard tool for combinatorial enumeration and asymptotic expansion."},"代数曲线":{"d":"The 1-dimensional algebraic variety defined by a polynomial in two variables F(x, y) = 0 (or by a homogeneous F(X,Y,Z) = 0 in the projective plane). If F is irreducible the curve is irreducible; if the gradient vanishes nowhere the curve is smooth.","t":"Smooth projective algebraic curves and compact Riemann surfaces form the same category; the genus is the only birational invariant, so the birational classification of curves is given entirely by the genus."},"代数曲面":{"d":"The 2-dimensional algebraic variety defined by a polynomial in three variables. Birational classification is given by the Kodaira dimension κ and the geometric genus p_g: κ = −∞ (rational, ruled), κ = 0 (K3, abelian, Enriques), κ = 1 (elliptic surfaces), κ = 2 (general type).","t":"Enriques–Kodaira classification: complex algebraic surfaces fall into ten classes according to Kodaira dimension; this is the prototype of higher-dimensional birational classification (the Mori program)."},"K3 曲面":{"d":"A simply connected compact complex surface with H⁰(X, Ω²) spanned by a nonzero holomorphic 2-form (that is, irregularity q = 0 and geometric genus p_g = 1). All K3 surfaces are diffeomorphic to one another, and diffeomorphic to a quartic surface in P³.","t":"The Hodge numbers are fixed as (h⁰⁰, h¹⁰, h¹¹, h²⁰, h²²) = (1, 0, 20, 0, 1); as a Calabi–Yau manifold it is a central object in string compactification."},"概型":{"d":"A scheme is a locally ringed space locally isomorphic to an affine scheme Spec R, where R is a commutative ring and Spec R is its set of prime ideals equipped with the Zariski topology and the structure sheaf. It replaces the solution set of equations by the prime spectrum of a ring, thereby unifying algebraic geometry and number theory in one language.","t":"Spec gives a contravariant equivalence: the category of affine schemes ≃ (the category of commutative rings)^op. A scheme is determined by its functor of points Hom(−, X) (Yoneda lemma)."},"Spec(R)":{"d":"Spec R is the set of all prime ideals of R, equipped with the Zariski topology defined by the closed sets V(I) = {𝔭 : 𝔭 ⊇ I} and with the structure sheaf O. The residue field at a point 𝔭 is k(𝔭) = Frac(R/𝔭); the generic point corresponds to the zero ideal and records an entire subvariety, while a closed point records only a single point.","t":"Spec gives a contravariant equivalence between the category of rings and the category of affine schemes: a ring homomorphism R → S corresponds to a morphism Spec S → Spec R, so the direction of arrows is reversed here; this is the foundation of scheme theory."},"流形 → 概型":{"d":"Replacing the condition locally isomorphic to Rⁿ by the condition locally isomorphic to Spec R moves one from the naive category of manifolds into the category of schemes. The cost is the intuition of points (points become prime ideals); the gain is that geometry can be done over an arbitrary ring, including arithmetic geometry over Z.","t":"This replacement turns integer solutions into sections of schemes over Z, and Fermat's last theorem thereby becomes a statement about schemes."},"三次曲面":{"d":"A smooth algebraic surface in P³ defined by an irreducible homogeneous polynomial of degree three. A smooth cubic surface contains exactly 27 lines (Cayley–Salmon theorem).","t":"The configuration of the 27 lines is one of the starting points of 19th-century algebraic geometry; a smooth cubic surface is rational and birationally equivalent to the blow-up of P² at six points."},"高次曲面":{"d":"The zero set in P³ of an irreducible homogeneous polynomial of degree d ≥ 4; it is a 2-dimensional projective algebraic variety. The degree determines its Hilbert polynomial and arithmetic genus.","t":"For d ≥ 5 a smooth surface is of general type; the quartic surface (d = 4) is special — it is exactly the model of a K3 surface."},"三阶张量":{"d":"A tensor of order three is a multilinear map T: V*×V*×V → K, written T^{ij}_k in coordinates, with three indices. Its order is defined by the number of indices, not by the dimension of the array.","t":"The Levi-Civita symbol ε_{ijk} is a tensor of order three (a tensor density); it writes the cross product and the determinant as one and the same thing: (a×b)_i = ε_{ijk}a_jb_k."},"四阶张量":{"d":"A tensor of order four is a multilinear map T: V*×V*×V×V → K, written T^{ij}_{kl} in coordinates, with four indices. It is not a four-dimensional array — only objects that transform by the covariant/contravariant rules under a change of basis are tensors.","t":"The Riemann curvature tensor R^{i}_{jkl} is a tensor of order four; for n = 4 its number of independent components is 20, obtained from 256 components by the Bianchi identities."},"泛函 / 算子":{"d":"A functional is a map from a vector space to the scalar field; an operator is a linear map between vector spaces. The set B(X) of bounded operators on a normed space X forms a Banach algebra under the operator norm.","t":"The four fundamental theorems: Hahn–Banach (extension), open mapping, closed graph, uniform boundedness (Banach–Steinhaus). All of them depend on completeness and all fail in a general metric space."},"球面 S²":{"d":"S² = {(x, y, z) ∈ R³ : x² + y² + z² = 1}, a 2-dimensional compact smooth manifold without boundary, with Euler characteristic χ = 2, simply connected.","t":"Gauss–Bonnet: ∮_S K dA = 2πχ(S²) = 4π. Total curvature is a topological invariant, independent of how the surface is bent."},"环面 T²":{"d":"T² = R²/Z², a compact surface without boundary of genus 1, with χ = 0 and fundamental group Z². Equipped with a complex structure it is an elliptic curve.","t":"The complex structure of the torus is given by the modulus τ taking values in the upper half-plane, and the moduli space is H/SL(2, Z) — the most classical moduli space and the starting point of the theory of modular forms."},"莫比乌斯带":{"d":"M = [0,1]×[0,1] / (0, y) ~ (1, 1−y). It is a non-orientable 2-dimensional manifold with boundary, and its boundary is a single circle, not two edges.","t":"Orientable ⇔ the first Stiefel–Whitney class w₁ = 0. The Möbius band is the smallest example with w₁ ≠ 0; its twist is exactly the transition function taking the nontrivial value in Z₂."},"椭球":{"d":"The closed surface defined by x²/a² + y²/b² + z²/c² = 1 (a, b, c > 0). It is the only compact quadric surface without boundary in R³.","t":"Its volume is 4πabc/3. When a, b, c are pairwise distinct, its isometry group is the finite group Z₂×Z₂×Z₂ — continuous symmetry occurs only in the sphere case (a=b=c)."},"尖点三次曲线":{"d":"The cuspidal cubic is the plane algebraic curve y² = x³, also the image of the map t ↦ (t², t³). At the origin both partial derivatives vanish simultaneously and the tangent cone degenerates to a double line; it is the standard model of a cusp singularity. The normalization map ℙ¹ → C is a continuous bijection, so its geometric genus is 0.","t":"The δ-invariant is δ = 1, and the genus formula g = (d−1)(d−2)/2 − Σδ gives g = 0: the singularity lowers the genus of the cubic from 1 to 0."},"尖点 y² = x³":{"d":"The curve y² = x³ has two branches that become tangent and merge at the origin, forming a cusp. There ∂F/∂x = ∂F/∂y = 0 and the gradient vanishes.","t":"At a cusp the tangent is unique but not smooth: the parametrization t ↦ (t², t³) is a bijection and continuous, but its inverse is not continuous — this is the analytic meaning of a singularity."},"曼德勃罗集":{"d":"M = {c ∈ C : the iteration z ↦ z² + c starting from z₀ = 0 stays bounded}. M is compact and connected, and its boundary is not a curve: Shishikura proved that the boundary has Hausdorff dimension 2.","t":"c ∈ M ⇔ the corresponding Julia set J_c is connected. This correspondence ties parameter space to dynamical system and is the central fact of complex dynamics."},"欧氏几何":{"d":"The transformation group is the isometry group E(2) = R² ⋊ O(2) generated by translations and orthogonal transformations. Euclidean geometry studies the invariants under the action of E(2).","t":"Isometries preserve distance, angle and area; conversely, a map preserving distance must be an isometry (Mazur–Ulam theorem). The invariants determine the classification completely."},"相似几何":{"d":"The similarity group Sim(2) = R² ⋊ (R₊ × O(2)), generated by translations, rotations and uniform scaling. The invariants are angles and ratios of lengths.","t":"A similarity multiplies every length by the same factor k, so absolute length loses meaning while ratios survive — this is the group-theoretic explanation of why similar triangles determine shape only."},"仿射几何":{"d":"The affine group A(2) = R² ⋊ GL(2, R), generated by translations and invertible linear transformations. The invariants are collinearity, parallelism and ratios of lengths along a single line.","t":"In affine geometry any triangle is equivalent to any other triangle, and any ellipse to a circle — enlarging the group coarsens the classification. Areas scale by |det|."},"射影几何":{"d":"The projective plane RP² = (R³ \\ {0}) / R*, with transformation group PGL(3, R). Projective geometry studies the properties that preserve the incidence relation of a point lying on a line.","t":"All non-degenerate conics are equivalent to one another under projective transformations; parallel lines meet at a point at infinity, so being parallel is no longer a projective invariant. The cross ratio is the fundamental invariant."},"共形几何":{"d":"A conformal transformation is one that preserves angles (that is, preserves the metric up to a positive function factor). On R² the conformal transformations are exactly the holomorphic or antiholomorphic functions; for n ≥ 3, Liouville's theorem leaves only Möbius transformations.","t":"A dimension dichotomy: for n = 2 the conformal group is infinite-dimensional, for n ≥ 3 it is finite-dimensional. Two-dimensional conformal field theory is possible precisely because of this infinite-dimensional symmetry."},"辛几何":{"d":"A symplectic manifold is a smooth manifold (M, ω) equipped with a closed, non-degenerate 2-form ω; its dimension must be even. A symplectomorphism is a diffeomorphism preserving ω.","t":"Darboux's theorem: locally every symplectic manifold is equivalent to (R²ⁿ, Σ dqᵢ ∧ dpᵢ). Symplectic geometry has no local invariants — a sharp contrast with Riemannian geometry, where curvature is a local invariant."},"切触几何":{"d":"A contact manifold is an odd-dimensional manifold equipped with a hyperplane field ξ = ker α satisfying α ∧ (dα)ⁿ ≠ 0 (completely non-integrable). A contact transformation is a diffeomorphism preserving ξ.","t":"The contact Darboux theorem: locally every contact structure is equivalent to the standard form dz − Σ yᵢdxᵢ = 0. As in symplectic geometry, there are no local invariants."},"微分拓扑":{"d":"The discipline studying invariants of smooth manifolds under diffeomorphism. Its core tools are transversality, Morse theory and cobordism.","t":"Milnor's exotic 7-spheres (1956) show that there exist manifolds that are homeomorphic but not diffeomorphic — topologically the same, smoothly different; this is the point where differential topology becomes independent of topology. The h-cobordism theorem (Smale) then gives the higher-dimensional classification."},"代数几何":{"d":"The geometry of algebraic varieties and schemes studied by the methods of commutative algebra. The basic correspondence is Hilbert's Nullstellensatz: algebraic varieties over k ↔ reduced finitely generated k-algebras.","t":"Three pillars: Nullstellensatz (the dictionary between algebra and geometry), Bézout (degree and intersection counting), Riemann–Roch (genus and divisors)."},"梯度 ∇φ":{"d":"For a scalar field φ: Rⁿ → R, the gradient is the vector field ∇φ = (∂φ/∂x₁, …, ∂φ/∂xₙ). Its direction is that of fastest increase of φ, and its modulus equals the directional derivative in that direction.","t":"∇φ is everywhere perpendicular to the level surface φ = const. This is the geometric basis of Lagrange multipliers: at a constrained extremum the gradient of the objective must be parallel to the gradient of the constraint."},"散度 ∇·F":{"d":"The divergence of a vector field F is ∇·F = Σ ∂Fᵢ/∂xᵢ, equal to the volume density of flux: ∇·F = lim_{V→0} (1/V) ∮_{∂V} F·n dS.","t":"Gauss divergence theorem: ∭_V ∇·F dV = ∬_{∂V} F·n dS. ∇·F = 0 means no sources and no sinks, equivalent to incompressibility."},"旋度 ∇×F":{"d":"The curl of a three-dimensional vector field F is the vector field ∇×F whose i-th component is ε_{ijk} ∂_j F_k. Its direction is the local axis of rotation and its modulus is twice the areal density of circulation.","t":"Stokes' theorem: ∬_S (∇×F)·n dS = ∮_{∂S} F·dr. ∇×F = 0 ⇔ locally a gradient field (conservative field)."},"应力张量与莫尔圆":{"d":"The Cauchy stress tensor σ is a symmetric tensor of order two; σ(n) = σ·n gives the surface force on the section with normal n. The principal stresses are the eigenvalues of σ and the principal directions its eigenvectors.","t":"The three invariants I₁ = tr σ, I₂, I₃ = det σ are independent of the coordinate system. Mohr's circle is the graphical method for a two-dimensional stress state: centre (σ₁+σ₂)/2, radius (σ₁−σ₂)/2, and σ₁+σ₂ is identically the trace — this is what invariance under rotation of axes means."},"两自由度模态与拍":{"d":"The equation of motion of a two-degree-of-freedom linear system is Mq̈ + Kq = 0, and its solution is given by the generalized eigenvalue problem Kφ = ω²Mφ: the ωᵢ are the natural frequencies and the φᵢ the modes (eigenvectors).","t":"When M and K are symmetric positive definite, the ωᵢ² are real and the φᵢ are orthogonal with respect to M — modal decomposition is decoupling by eigenbasis coordinates. When the two modal frequencies are close, their superposition produces beats with envelope frequency |ω₁ − ω₂|/2."},"相空间轨道":{"d":"Phase space is the space of positions and momenta (q, p) ∈ R^{2n}. The Hamilton equations q̇ = ∂H/∂p, ṗ = −∂H/∂q define a vector field on it, and the orbits are integral curves.","t":"Liouville's theorem: Hamiltonian flow preserves phase volume, so orbits can neither converge nor diverge — this is the dynamical version of incompressibility and the foundation of statistical mechanics."},"李代数与指数映射":{"d":"A Lie algebra g is a vector space equipped with a Lie bracket [·,·] (antisymmetric and satisfying the Jacobi identity). The Lie algebra of a Lie group G is g = T_eG; the exponential map exp: g → G sends generators back to group elements.","t":"Baker–Campbell–Hausdorff formula: exp X · exp Y = exp(X + Y + ½[X,Y] + ⋯). The non-commutativity of the group is encoded entirely by the bracket (that is, by the correction of first order)."},"纤维丛与扭转":{"d":"A fibre bundle is a map π: E → B that is locally trivial (π⁻¹(U) ≅ U × F) and whose transition functions take values in the structure group G. The twist is described by the G-valued Čech cohomology class of the transition functions.","t":"The Möbius band is a nontrivial bundle with base S¹, fibre an interval and structure group Z₂; its nontriviality is exactly w₁ ≠ 0, and it is the topological reason why one full twist returns to the starting point only after two turns."},"分数与小数":{"d":"The fraction a/b (a, b ∈ Z, b ≠ 0) and a decimal are two notations for the same rational number. Criterion: if the denominator of the fraction in lowest terms has only the prime factors 2 and 5, it can be written as a finite decimal; otherwise it must be an infinite repeating decimal.","t":"Rational ⇔ finite decimal or infinite repeating decimal. This criterion exposes the necessity of extending the number system: the gap occupied by the infinite non-repeating decimals is exactly where the irrational numbers sit."},"周长与面积":{"d":"The perimeter is the length of the boundary curve, and the area is the Lebesgue measure of the enclosed region (in the elementary case, the additive extension of rectangle area). The two have different dimensions, and there is no general relation between them.","t":"Isoperimetric inequality: for fixed perimeter the circle has the largest area, L² ≥ 4πS, with equality only for the circle. This shows that the only universal constraint between perimeter and area is an inequality, not an equation."},"用字母表示数":{"d":"Abstracting concrete numbers into variables yields algebraic expressions. The laws of operation (commutative, associative, distributive) are the legitimate basis for identical transformations; a letter does not stand for an unknown but for an arbitrary number.","t":"Once letters stand for numbers, algorithm and result separate: ax + b is a program, and only substituting a, b, x gives a number. This is the dividing line between algebra and arithmetic."},"找规律与正比例":{"d":"A direct proportion is y = kx (k ≠ 0): the ratio of the two quantities is constant. Geometrically its graph is a line through the origin, and k is the slope.","t":"Direct proportion is the special case of a linear function with zero intercept; once an intercept is allowed, y = kx + b no longer preserves ratios and preserves only difference quotients — this is the difference between linear and proportional."},"数对与坐标系":{"d":"The rectangular coordinate system in the plane sets up a one-to-one correspondence between R² and points: point ↔ ordered pair (x, y). Ordered is the key — (1, 2) and (2, 1) are different points.","t":"Coordinates translate geometry into equations; this is the entire premise of analytic geometry. Descartes' core contribution is not drawing figures but this translation rule."},"统计三中心":{"d":"The mean is x̄ = Σxᵢ/n; the median is the middle value after sorting (for an even number of values, the average of the two middle ones); the mode is the value occurring most often. The three are defined differently and answer different questions.","t":"Σ(xᵢ − x̄) = 0: the deviations sum to zero, a property unique to the mean, satisfied by neither the median nor the mode. The mean is sensitive to outliers (it is not a robust statistic), while the median is robust."},"无理数与实数":{"d":"An irrational number is a real number that cannot be written as a/b, equivalently one whose decimal expansion is infinite and non-repeating. The classical proof of the irrationality of √2 is by contradiction: if √2 = p/q in lowest terms, then p² = 2q², which forces p and q both even, contradicting lowest terms.","t":"The reals = rationals + irrationals, and the irrationals are uncountable (Cantor's diagonal argument) while the rationals are countable. In terms of quantity, almost all real numbers are irrational."},"一元二次方程":{"d":"ax² + bx + c = 0 (a ≠ 0). There are three routes to the solution — factoring, completing the square, the quadratic formula — and they are algebraically equivalent: completing the square turns the general form into (x + b/2a)² = (b²−4ac)/4a², and the quadratic formula is its solution.","t":"The discriminant Δ = b² − 4ac decides the number of real roots; Vieta's formulas x₁ + x₂ = −b/a, x₁x₂ = c/a connect the solutions directly with the coefficients and are the tool for all later symmetric polynomial problems."},"勾股定理":{"d":"In the Euclidean plane, the two legs a, b and the hypotenuse c of a right triangle satisfy a² + b² = c². This is equivalent to the inner product space property: two vectors are perpendicular ⇔ their inner product is zero.","t":"The converse holds: a² + b² = c² ⇒ right angle. The Pythagorean theorem occupies the core position in Euclidean geometry because it defines distance — in hyperbolic or spherical geometry the same relation becomes an expression involving cosh or cos."},"一次与二次函数":{"d":"The first-degree function y = kx + b (graph a line, k the slope, b the intercept); the second-degree function y = ax² + bx + c (a ≠ 0, graph a parabola, axis of symmetry x = −b/2a, vertex form y = a(x + b/2a)² + (4ac−b²)/4a).","t":"The three forms of a quadratic function (general, vertex, intercept) each expose different information: the general form shows the coefficients, the vertex form the extremum, the intercept form the zeros. One object, three coordinate systems."},"二元一次方程组":{"d":"Two linear equations taken together. Geometrically it is the intersection point of two lines. Elimination and substitution are essentially the same: both perform invertible row operations.","t":"The number of solutions is decided by the rank of the coefficient matrix and the rank of the augmented matrix: equal ranks equal to the number of unknowns gives a unique solution, equal but smaller gives infinitely many, unequal gives none. This criterion is exactly the same for n unknowns."},"频率与概率":{"d":"Frequency is the ratio of the number of occurrences of an event in an experiment to the total number of trials, and it varies with the experiment; probability is a measure P defined on a σ-algebra of events satisfying P(Ω) = 1 and countable additivity. Frequency converges to probability by the law of large numbers.","t":"Law of large numbers (Bernoulli): as n → ∞ the frequency converges in probability to p. This theorem is the only basis for saying that probability can be measured."},"数列":{"d":"A sequence is a function a: N* → R defined on the positive integers, written {aₙ}. Arithmetic: aₙ₊₁ − aₙ = d; geometric: aₙ₊₁/aₙ = q.","t":"Sₙ is a quadratic function of n with zero constant term ⇔ {aₙ} is arithmetic; aₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁ is the basic bridge between a sequence and its sum. The general solution of the first-order linear recurrence aₙ₊₁ = paₙ + q is determined by the fixed point q/(1−p)."},"三角函数":{"d":"On the unit circle, the terminal side of the angle θ meets the circle at (cos θ, sin θ). Hence sin²θ + cos²θ = 1; in general form y = A sin(ωx + φ) + k, with amplitude |A|, angular frequency ω, period 2π/|ω| and initial phase φ.","t":"The addition formula sin(α ± β) = sin α cos β ± cos α sin β is the source of all trigonometric identities: setting β = α gives the double-angle formula, dividing gives the tan form, adding and subtracting gives sum-to-product."},"圆锥曲线":{"d":"The set of points in the plane determined by the general quadratic equation Ax² + Bxy + Cy² + Dx + Ey + F = 0. According to the sign of B² − 4AC the conic is of elliptic (<0), parabolic (=0) or hyperbolic (>0) type; when non-degenerate these are respectively an ellipse, a parabola and a hyperbola. The unified description is: the ratio of the distance to the focus to the distance to the directrix equals e, with e<1 ellipse, e=1 parabola, e>1 hyperbola.","t":"In the projective plane all non-degenerate conics are equivalent to one another — they are the same object seen in different affine slices. This is the exact meaning of the statement that circles, ellipses, parabolas and hyperbolas belong to one family."},"复数与复平面":{"d":"C = {a + bi : a, b ∈ R}, i² = −1. Geometrically a complex number is a plane vector, and multiplication is multiply the moduli and add the arguments — multiplying by i is a counterclockwise rotation through 90°.","t":"Fundamental theorem of algebra: every polynomial of degree n over C has exactly n roots (counted with multiplicity), so C is algebraically closed while R is not. The price is that no total order compatible with the field structure can be defined on C."},"矩阵与行列式初步":{"d":"A matrix is the coordinate representation of a linear transformation with respect to a basis; the determinant is its signed volume scaling factor: |det A| equals the volume of the image of the unit cube, and the sign says whether orientation is reversed.","t":"det(AB) = det A · det B shows that the determinant is a homomorphism from multiplication to multiplication; invertible ⇔ det ≠ 0. The 2×2 determinant ad − bc is exactly the area of a parallelogram."},"分布列与期望":{"d":"The distribution table of a discrete random variable X is pₖ = P(X = xₖ), satisfying pₖ ≥ 0 and Σpₖ = 1. The expectation is E[X] = Σ xₖpₖ and the variance Var X = E[(X − EX)²] = E[X²] − (EX)².","t":"Linearity of expectation E[X + Y] = EX + EY holds unconditionally (independence is not needed), whereas Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y requires uncorrelatedness. This distinction is the most frequently misused point in probability theory."},"实数完备与数系结构":{"d":"R is an ordered field satisfying the completeness axiom: every nonempty subset bounded above has a least upper bound. ℕ ⊂ ℤ ⊂ ℚ ⊂ ℝ ⊂ ℂ is a layer-by-layer extension, and each extension gives up one property — ℚ → ℝ gives up countability, ℝ → ℂ gives up order.","t":"Equivalent characterizations of completeness: the least upper bound principle, the monotone bounded theorem, nested closed intervals, Bolzano–Weierstrass, convergence of Cauchy sequences — all are equivalent. R is the unique complete ordered field (up to isomorphism)."},"线性方程组解的结构":{"d":"The solution set of Ax = b is particular solution plus homogeneous solution space: x = x₀ + ker A. The dimension of ker A equals n − rank A (rank–nullity theorem).","t":"Rank–Nullity: dim ker A + rank A = n. Consistency criterion: rank A = rank [A|b]. Together these two statements describe the structure of the solution set completely, with no further case distinction."},"空间解析几何":{"d":"The study by coordinates of points, lines, planes and surfaces in R³: a plane is given by a linear equation, a line by the intersection of two planes (or in parametric form), a surface by an implicit equation F(x,y,z) = 0.","t":"Normal vectors and direction vectors are the entry point of every computation: point-plane distance, line-plane angle, plane-plane angle all reduce to vector projection and angle. Quadric surfaces are then classified by quadratic forms."},"极限 · 导数 · 积分 · 级数":{"d":"Limit: the ε-δ definition, lim_{x→a} f(x) = L means that for every ε>0 there is δ>0 such that 0<|x−a|<δ implies |f(x)−L|<ε. Derivative: f′(a) = lim_{h→0} (f(a+h)−f(a))/h. Definite integral: the limit of Riemann sums. Series: the limit of the sequence of partial sums.","t":"Fundamental theorem of calculus: if f is continuous, then F(x) = ∫ₐˣ f is differentiable and F′ = f — differentiation and integration are inverse operations. This single statement merges the tangent problem and the area problem into one theorem."},"线性代数":{"d":"A vector space is a set equipped with addition and scalar multiplication satisfying eight axioms; a linear map preserves these two operations. Once a basis is chosen, a linear map corresponds to a matrix, and a change of basis corresponds to similarity.","t":"Spectral theorem: a real symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalized, A = QΛQᵀ, and all its eigenvalues are real. The Jordan normal form gives the complete classification in the non-diagonalizable case. SVD factors an arbitrary matrix as rotation–scaling–rotation."},"概率论与数理统计":{"d":"A probability space is a triple (Ω, F, P), where F is a σ-algebra and P is a measure satisfying countable additivity with P(Ω) = 1. A random variable is a measurable function. Statistics goes the other way, from sample back to the population distribution, by means of estimators and test statistics.","t":"Central limit theorem: for independent identically distributed variables with finite variance, (S̄ₙ − μ)/(σ/√n) converges in distribution to N(0,1). This theorem is the answer to why the normal distribution is everywhere, and the basis of all interval estimation and hypothesis testing."},"一次":{"d":"A polynomial of degree one: f(x) = ax + b (a ≠ 0). The zero is unique, the graph is a line, and the derivative is the constant a.","t":"Degree one is the starting point of solvability: every linear equation has a unique solution, and the solution can be written with the four operations."},"二次":{"d":"A polynomial of degree two: f(x) = ax² + bx + c (a ≠ 0). There are at most two zeros, the graph is a parabola, and the derivative is a function of degree one.","t":"Degree two is where the notion of discriminant is born: the number of solutions is decided by the sign of Δ, and this pattern continues in higher degree as the discriminant being a polynomial in the coefficients."},"三次":{"d":"A cubic polynomial f(x) = ax³ + bx² + cx + d (a ≠ 0). It has at least one real root (an odd-degree polynomial must change sign), and the two ends of its graph tend to opposite infinities.","t":"The cubic is the boundary at which complex numbers become unavoidable: casus irreducibilis shows that in the case of three real roots the solution by radicals must still contain imaginary numbers."},"五次":{"d":"The general equation of the fifth degree. Its Galois group is S₅, and S₅ is not solvable, so no general solution by radicals exists.","t":"Abel–Ruffini: the general equation of degree n ≥ 5 has no solution by radicals. Individual quintics may still be solvable, and the criterion is the solvability of their Galois group."},"1 元 1 次":{"d":"One unknown, one first-degree equation: ax = b. The solution set is a single point, the empty set, or the whole space, according to whether a is zero.","t":"These three cases correspond exactly to the comparison of the rank of the coefficient matrix with the rank of the augmented matrix; this is the smallest model for the classification of linear systems."},"2 元 1 次":{"d":"Two unknowns, one first-degree equation: Ax + By = C. The solution set is a line in the plane — one equation cannot single out a point, only a 1-dimensional set.","t":"The dimension of the solution set = the number of unknowns − the number of independent equations. This rule holds for every n and every degree (the dimension of an algebraic variety)."},"3 元 1 次":{"d":"Three unknowns, one first-degree equation: Ax + By + Cz = D. The solution set is a plane in R³, 2-dimensional.","t":"From 1 unknown to n unknowns, the solution set of a first-degree equation rises from a point to an (n−1)-dimensional hyperplane — adding one unknown raises the dimension of the solution set by exactly one."},"4 元 1 次":{"d":"Four unknowns, one first-degree equation. The solution set is a 3-dimensional hyperplane in R⁴; it cannot be drawn directly and can only be projected.","t":"Beyond dimension 3 the solution set no longer has an intuitive picture, yet it is still described completely by linear algebra — this is precisely the value of the coordinate method."},"离心率":{"d":"The eccentricity e of a conic section is the ratio of the distance to the focus to the distance to the directrix. In one and the same equation only e is changed: e = 0 a circle, 0 < e < 1 an ellipse, e = 1 a parabola, e > 1 a hyperbola.","t":"e is the only shape parameter of the family of conic sections (the other parameter is merely scale). One number varying continuously strings four different curves into a single orbit."},"对称互换":{"d":"Interchanging x and y, that is, reflecting in the line y = x. This transformation of the graph corresponds to finding the inverse function — if (a, b) lies on the original graph, then (b, a) lies on the graph of the inverse function.","t":"A function is invertible ⇔ it is strictly monotone (on an interval). After the interchange the domain and the range are interchanged as well, and this is where mistakes with inverse functions most often occur."},"复数求逆":{"d":"1/z = z̄/|z|² (z ≠ 0). Geometrically, taking the reciprocal means taking the reciprocal of the modulus and the negative of the argument — it maps the inside of a circle to the outside, and is an inversion transformation.","t":"z ↦ 1/z is a special case of a Möbius transformation; it maps generalized circles (circles and lines) again to generalized circles. It is the basic building block of conformal mappings in complex analysis."},"ℚ → ℝ":{"d":"Two standard routes from ℚ to ℝ: the Dedekind cut (cutting ℚ into a left and a right half) and completion by Cauchy sequences (adjoining all limit points). The two routes give isomorphic fields.","t":"The price is uncountability: ℚ is countable, ℝ is not. At the same time something appears in ℝ that is absent from ℚ — the irrational numbers, and they are the overwhelming majority."},"ℝ → ℂ":{"d":"ℂ = ℝ[i]/(i² + 1), that is, adjoining to ℝ an element satisfying i² = −1. It is the algebraic closure of ℝ: every polynomial factors completely over ℂ.","t":"The price is the loss of order: no total order on ℂ is compatible with the field operations (otherwise −1 < 0 would contradict i² = −1). The gain is the fundamental theorem of algebra."},"五层数系":{"d":"ℕ → ℤ → ℚ → ℝ → ℂ; each layer solves one thing the layer below cannot do: subtraction, division, limits, root extraction. Each extension gives up one property.","t":"ℕ has no additive inverse → ℤ; ℤ has no division → ℚ; ℚ is not complete → ℝ; ℝ is not algebraically closed → ℂ. To extend beyond ℂ (to the quaternions) the price is the loss of commutativity of multiplication."},"x²−1 → x²+1":{"d":"Changing the constant term from −1 to +1: x² − 1 = 0 has two real roots ±1, while x² + 1 = 0 has none. The discriminant Δ = −4ac changes from positive to negative.","t":"The disappearance of the real roots does not mean there is no solution — the solutions have moved to the complex plane and become ±i. This step is where complex numbers are forced onto the scene."},"b 变号":{"d":"Changing the sign of the coefficient b of the first-degree term: ax² + bx + c and ax² − bx + c. The sum of the two roots changes from −b/a to b/a, that is, the two roots exchange signs (the product is unchanged).","t":"b → −b corresponds to reflecting the graph in the y-axis. One sign change in a coefficient appears in the solutions as a single overall symmetry — this is the direct reading of Vieta's formulas."},"sin 与 cos":{"d":"cos θ = sin(θ + π/2): the difference between the two is only a phase shift, not two different functions. On the graph, cos is sin shifted left by a quarter period.","t":"Every sinusoidal function can be written uniformly as A sin(ωx + φ); the statement that sin and cos are two curves is therefore an illusion produced by the choice of coordinates."},"交叉项变号":{"d":"In Ax² + Bxy + Cy², changing the sign of B is equivalent to replacing x by −x (or y by −y). This reverses the sign of the cross term of the quadratic form and thereby changes the direction of the principal axes.","t":"Whether a cross term is present is a question of how well the coordinates are chosen, not a property of the curve itself: every quadratic form can be freed of its cross term by a rotation (principal axis theorem)."},"ODE 解曲线":{"d":"The solution of the first-order equation y′ = f(x, y) is an integral curve: each point is assigned a slope and the solution follows it. <b>Picard–Lindelöf theorem</b>: if f satisfies a Lipschitz condition in y, then for a given initial value (x₀,y₀) the solution exists and is unique on some interval.","t":"Uniqueness is only local: y′ = y² starting from y(0)=1 blows up at x=1 — the solution reaches infinity in finite time."},"PDE 热 · 波 · 拉普拉斯":{"d":"Second-order linear PDEs fall into three classes according to the symbol of the principal part: parabolic (u_t = u_xx, heat equation), hyperbolic (u_tt = u_xx, wave equation), elliptic (Δu = 0, Laplace). The classification rests on the quadratic form of the principal symbol: degenerate, indefinite, definite.","t":"The three classes have entirely different properties: the heat equation smooths instantly (irreversible), the wave equation propagates along characteristics (reversible), and the Laplace equation has no time, its solution being determined uniquely by the boundary."},"Maxwell · Navier–Stokes":{"d":"Maxwell: ∇·E = ρ/ε₀, ∇·B = 0, ∇×E = −∂B/∂t, ∇×B = μ₀J + μ₀ε₀∂E/∂t. Navier–Stokes: ∂u/∂t + (u·∇)u = −∇p/ρ + νΔu, ∇·u = 0.","t":"Maxwell is linear and solvable; Navier–Stokes is nonlinear, and the existence and smoothness of solutions in three dimensions remain a Millennium Problem."},"变分 · 路径积分":{"d":"The calculus of variations seeks the extrema of a functional S[y] = ∫L(x, y, y′)dx; the necessary condition δS = 0 gives the Euler–Lagrange equation ∂L/∂y − d/dx(∂L/∂y′) = 0. The path integral sums e^{iS/ħ} over all paths.","t":"The Euler–Lagrange equation is the chain rule in its variational form; as ħ → 0 the phase oscillates violently, the contributions cancel one another, and only the classical path survives — this is the correspondence principle."},"ax + b":{"d":"A first-degree polynomial. It is the basic object of the arithmetic layer: written with the four operations alone, with no radicals and no transcendental functions.","t":""},"有理函数 P/Q":{"d":"The quotient P(x)/Q(x) of two polynomials. At the zeros of Q the denominator vanishes, and the curve breaks or develops a vertical asymptote.","t":"The integral of a rational function can always be expressed in elementary functions (partial fraction decomposition); this is the fundamental difference between it and irrational functions."},"连分式嵌套":{"d":"An expression of the form a₀ + b₁/(a₁ + b₂/(a₂ + ⋯)). Each level is nested inside the one above; D = 2 refers to exactly this kind of two-level nesting.","t":""},"无理数的连分式":{"d":"Every real number has a unique simple continued fraction expansion; it is finite for rationals and infinite for irrationals. The convergents 1/1, 3/2, 7/5, 17/12 of √2 = [1; 2, 2, 2, …] approach alternately and never arrive.","t":"The continued fraction of a quadratic irrational is necessarily periodic, and conversely (Lagrange) — this is the first example of periodicity in number theory."},"√x":{"d":"The square root, that is, the 1/2 power of x. It cannot be written with finitely many applications of the four operations, so it goes beyond L0 into L1 (the algebraic layer).","t":""},"√(x²+1)":{"d":"A quadratic expression inside a radical. Its graph is the upper branch of the hyperbola y² − x² = 1.","t":"This function is the integrand in ∫dx/√(x²+1) = arsinh x — algebraic functions and elementary transcendental functions join up here."},"迭代根式 √(1+√x)":{"d":"A radical inside a radical, nesting depth D = 2. The iterated √(1+√(1+⋯)) converges to the golden ratio φ = (1+√5)/2.","t":"The infinite iterated radical is the fixed point of the equation x = √(1+x), and that equation is precisely x² − x − 1 = 0."},"代数数的收敛":{"d":"An algebraic number is a root of some polynomial with integer coefficients. Newton iteration x ← (x + 2/x)/2 squares the error at each step and reaches the double precision limit in a few steps.","t":"Algebraic numbers are countable, transcendental numbers are not — so almost all numbers are roots of no equation."},"eˣ · sin x · ln x":{"d":"Three basic elementary transcendental functions. They cannot be expressed by the four operations + radicals, and they are the starting point of the L2 layer. Their domains differ: eˣ on the whole axis, sin on the whole axis, ln only for x > 0.","t":"The three are linked by Euler's formula e^{ix} = cos x + i sin x — the exponential and the trigonometric functions are originally two projections of one and the same function on the complex plane."},"e^{sin x}":{"d":"An exponential over a trigonometric function: e^{sin x} keeps the period 2π, but its values are confined to [1/e, e].","t":""},"ln(cos(eˣ))":{"d":"A trigonometric function over an exponential: the frequency of cos(eˣ) grows exponentially with x, and the oscillations become denser to the right, so that no finite resolution can draw it.","t":"This is the smallest example of a composite in which the frequency changes itself — and also the form most prone to error in numerical integration."},"泰勒 · 傅里叶级数":{"d":"Taylor series approximate by polynomials (locally), Fourier series by trigonometric functions (globally, for periodic functions). Both are limiting forms of infinite nesting.","t":"Taylor demands analyticity, Fourier only integrability — so Fourier series apply to far more functions, but their convergence is more delicate (Gibbs phenomenon)."},"Ai(x)　Airy":{"d":"The Airy function is a solution of y″ = x y, introduced by George Airy in his study of rainbow optics. It decays exponentially for x > 0 and oscillates for x < 0.","t":"It is the standard model of the turning point problem: on one and the same curve, oscillation on one side and decay on the other, with a smooth transition in between. The WKB approximation of quantum mechanics fails here."},"ζ(s)　Riemann":{"d":"The Riemann ζ function: ζ(s) = Σ n^{−s} (Re s > 1), extended by analytic continuation to the whole complex plane, with a single pole at s = 1.","t":"The Euler product ζ(s) = ∏(1−p^{−s})^{−1} encodes the primes in a single complex function — this is the starting point of analytic number theory. The Riemann hypothesis states that all nontrivial zeros lie on Re s = 1/2."},"模形式的基本域":{"d":"A modular form is a holomorphic function on the upper half-plane satisfying f((aτ+b)/(cτ+d)) = (cτ+d)^k f(τ). It is determined completely by its values on the fundamental domain {|Re τ| ≤ ½, |τ| ≥ 1} of SL(2,Z).","t":"The fundamental domain is a single brick: the whole upper half-plane is tiled by it under modular transformations. Studying modular forms therefore requires studying only this small piece — an extreme case of the local determining the global."},"₂F₁ 超几何":{"d":"The hypergeometric function ₂F₁(a,b;c;z) = Σ (a)ₙ(b)ₙ/(c)ₙ · zⁿ/n! is the generating function of a large class of special functions. For a=b=1, c=2 it degenerates to −ln(1−z)/z.","t":"Almost all elementary and special functions are special cases of the hypergeometric function — it is the unifying framework of the special function layer."},"Dirac δ":{"d":"δ is not a function but a distribution (generalized function): it satisfies ∫δ(x)φ(x)dx = φ(0). It is usually approximated by a family of peaks of constant area 1 whose width tends to 0.","t":"The derivative of δ is a dipole and the integral of δ is a step — the calculus of distributions gives a rigorous definition to such physical objects as point charges and instantaneous impulses."},"变分导数 δF/δy":{"d":"The variational derivative of a functional F[y] is defined as the derivative of F[y+εδy] with respect to ε at ε=0. It is the derivative obtained after treating the whole curve as a single variable.","t":"δF/δy = 0 is the Euler–Lagrange equation — everything in the calculus of variations comes from this one equation."},"Navier–Stokes 弱解":{"d":"A weak solution is required to satisfy the equation only in the integral sense, not to be differentiable pointwise: for every test function φ, ∫(u·∇φ)u + ν∫∇u·∇φ = ∫fφ.","t":"Existence of weak solutions (Leray 1934) is settled, but whether solutions in three dimensions are smooth and unique is still open — one of the Millennium Problems."},"路径积分":{"d":"The path integral writes the propagator as an integral over all paths, ∫e^{iS[y]/ħ}𝒟y. It is not a Lebesgue integral but a formal limit.","t":"As ħ → 0 the phase oscillates violently and the non-extremal paths cancel one another, leaving only the classical path — this is the origin of the principle of least action."},"Hilbert 空间":{"d":"A Hilbert space is a complete inner product space: every Cauchy sequence converges. In the separable case there is a countable orthonormal basis {eₙ}, every element expands uniquely as v = Σ⟨v,eₙ⟩eₙ, and Parseval's identity ‖v‖² = Σ|⟨v,eₙ⟩|² holds.","t":"All infinite-dimensional separable Hilbert spaces are isometrically isomorphic to one another; the closed unit ball is compact if and only if the space is finite-dimensional (Riesz's theorem), so in the infinite-dimensional case compactness must be replaced by weak compactness."},"Sobolev 流形":{"d":"The Sobolev space W^{k,p}(Ω) consists of all functions whose weak derivatives up to order k lie in Lᵖ(Ω), with the norm ‖u‖_{k,p} = (Σ_{|α|≤k}‖D^α u‖_p^p)^{1/p}. It is complete, and a space of maps modelled on it is an infinite-dimensional manifold.","t":"Sobolev embedding theorem: if k > n/p, then W^{k,p}(Ω) embeds continuously in C⁰(Ω); the larger k − n/p, the smoother the target space, and this is the basis for the regularization of weak solutions."},"算术曲面":{"d":"An arithmetic surface is an integral scheme X → Spec ℤ, proper and flat, of relative dimension 1. Its generic fibre is a smooth curve over ℚ, the fibre at a prime p is a curve over 𝔽_p, and the integral points of X correspond exactly to the sections of this fibration.","t":"If X → Spec ℤ has a section, its image gives infinitely many integral points; conversely, the existence of integral points can be read as the existence of a section over some open set — this is the basic translation that geometrizes Diophantine problems."},"双曲紧流形":{"d":"A hyperbolic manifold is a complete Riemannian manifold of constant sectional curvature −1, equivalently the quotient Γ\\ℍⁿ of hyperbolic space ℍⁿ by a discrete torsion-free subgroup Γ of isometries. In the Poincaré disk model of a compact hyperbolic surface the geodesics are exactly the circular arcs orthogonal to the boundary circle.","t":"Mostow rigidity: a complete finite-volume hyperbolic manifold of dimension n ≥ 3 is determined uniquely by its fundamental group — homotopy equivalence implies isometry — so its moduli space is discrete."},"PDE 的整体吸引子":{"d":"Let {S(t)}_{t≥0} be a dissipative semigroup on a Banach space X. Its global attractor 𝒜 is a compact invariant set such that dist(S(t)B, 𝒜) → 0 for every bounded set B; equivalently, 𝒜 is the closure of the union of the values at t = 0 of all bounded complete orbits.","t":"If the semigroup has a bounded absorbing set and is asymptotically compact, then the global attractor exists and is invariant; for equations such as the two-dimensional Navier–Stokes both the Hausdorff and the fractal dimension of 𝒜 are finite."},"椭圆纤维化曲面":{"d":"An elliptic surface is a compact complex surface X together with a surjective morphism π: X → B (B a smooth curve) such that the generic fibre is an elliptic curve and only finitely many fibres are degenerate. The degenerate fibres over the discriminant locus are classified by Kodaira into types I_n, II, III, IV, I_n*, etc., each class having a definite local equation.","t":"The Kodaira formula K_X = π*(K_B + Δ) writes the canonical class of the surface as the sum of the contribution of the base curve and that of the degenerate fibres; this is the entry point to the Enriques–Kodaira classification."},"路径纤维化 ΩM":{"d":"Let M be a based topological space, PM the space of all paths γ: [0,1] → M, and ΩM the space of loops with γ(0) = γ(1) = x₀. The evaluation map PM → M, γ ↦ γ(1), is a fibration with fibre ΩM, and PM is contractible.","t":"The long exact homotopy sequence of the fibration gives π_n(ΩM) ≅ π_{n+1}(M); for n = 0 this gives π₀(ΩM) ≅ π₁(M), that is, the fundamental group is the set of connected components of the loop space."},"代数叠（Artin stack）":{"d":"An algebraic stack is a category fibred in groupoids X → (Sch/S)_{fppf} satisfying descent, whose diagonal is representable and for which there exists a smooth surjection U → X with U a scheme. Each of its points carries an automorphism group (stabilizer), so what it records is the whole moduli functor rather than a set of isomorphism classes.","t":"Artin's theorem: a moduli functor satisfying Schlessinger's deformation conditions is represented by an Artin stack; when the cover is taken étale the stack is called a Deligne–Mumford stack."},"黎曼面模空间 M_g":{"d":"M_g is the space of equivalence classes of compact Riemann surfaces of genus g under biholomorphism; it is smooth as a Deligne–Mumford stack and a quasi-projective variety as a coarse moduli space. For g ≥ 2, dim_ℂ M_g = 3g − 3; for g = 1 it is 1, and for g = 0 it is a single point.","t":"Teichmüller space T_g is contractible and the modular subgroup Γ_g acts on it, with M_g = T_g/Γ_g; hence M_g is an orbifold and not a manifold."},"椭圆曲线的 j-线":{"d":"For the lattice Λ_τ = ℤ + ℤτ, the j-invariant of E_τ = ℂ/Λ_τ is j(τ) = 1728·4a³/(4a³ + 27b²), with a, b coming from the Weierstrass equation. j is a modular function for SL(2,ℤ), and two curves are isomorphic if and only if their j are equal.","t":"j induces a biholomorphism ℍ/SL(2,ℤ) ≅ ℂ, with j(i) = 1728 and j(ρ) = 0, corresponding respectively to the two curves whose automorphism groups are ℤ/4 and ℤ/6."},"Yang–Mills 瞬子":{"d":"Let P → ℝ⁴ be a principal bundle with structure group a compact Lie group G, A a connection and F_A its curvature. An instanton is a connection satisfying the self-duality equation F_A = *F_A with finite energy ∫|F_A|² < ∞; its topological charge is k = −(1/8π²)∫ tr(F_A ∧ F_A).","t":"The ADHM construction identifies the moduli space of SU(2) k-instantons with the solutions of a system of matrix equations modulo the action of U(k); this moduli space is smooth of dimension 8k − 3."},"代数叠的模空间":{"d":"Every algebraic stack X has a coarse moduli space π: X → X_coarse, where X_coarse is a scheme, π induces a one-to-one correspondence of connected components, and every morphism from X to a scheme factors uniquely through π. Coarsening amounts to forgetting the automorphism group at each point.","t":"Coarsening is not an isomorphism invariant of stacks: a nontrivial μ_n-gerbe has the same coarse moduli space as its base scheme, so the coarse space cannot recover the stack, and two different stacks may share one coarse space."},"二项式定理":{"d":"For x, y in a commutative ring and n a positive integer, (x+y)ⁿ = Σ_{k=0}^{n} C(n,k)·xᵏ·y^{n−k}, where C(n,k) = n!/(k!(n−k)!) is the number of k-element subsets of an n-element set. The coefficients form the n-th row of Pascal's triangle.","t":"Pascal's identity C(n,k) = C(n−1,k) + C(n−1,k−1) follows by comparing coefficients in (1+x)ⁿ = (1+x)(1+x)^{n−1}."},"三项式定理":{"d":"For x, y, z in a commutative ring and n a positive integer, (x+y+z)ⁿ = Σ_{i+j+k=n} (n!/(i!j!k!))·xⁱ·yʲ·zᵏ, the sum running over all triples of non-negative integers with i + j + k = n. The coefficients form a three-dimensional Pascal pyramid.","t":"The coefficient n!/(i!j!k!) equals the number of ways of splitting n distinct elements into three groups of sizes i, j, k; setting x = y = z = 1 gives 3ⁿ as the sum of all coefficients."},"多项式定理":{"d":"For x₁, …, x_m in a commutative ring and n a positive integer, (x₁+⋯+x_m)ⁿ = Σ (n!/(i₁!⋯i_m!))·x₁^{i₁}⋯x_m^{i_m}, the sum running over all tuples of non-negative integers with i₁ + ⋯ + i_m = n; the coefficients are the multinomial coefficients.","t":"Setting all x_j = 1 gives Σ n!/(i₁!⋯i_m!) = mⁿ; m = 2 and m = 3 give back the binomial and trinomial theorems, which are therefore special cases."},"一次型":{"d":"A linear form is a linear map f(x) = Σ aᵢxᵢ = a·x whose coefficients a = (a₁, …, a_n) form a vector, that is, a covariant tensor of order one. The zero set {x : a·x = 0} is a hyperplane through the origin and a is its normal vector, so linear forms correspond one-to-one with hyperplanes.","t":"The level sets f = c of a linear form are a family of mutually parallel hyperplanes; on an affine space the graph of a linear function is a hyperplane whose gradient equals the constant vector a everywhere."},"d 次型":{"d":"A form of degree d is F(x) = Σ t_{i₁⋯i_d}·x_{i₁}⋯x_{i_d}, its coefficients t being a symmetric tensor of order d. Over a field of characteristic 0 the polarization formula gives mutually inverse correspondences between forms of degree d and symmetric d-linear forms, so the two are two ways of writing the same object.","t":"When 0 < d < char k, the space of forms of degree d and the space of symmetric tensors of order d are isomorphic by polarization, both of dimension C(n+d−1, d); for d = 2 this is the correspondence between quadratic forms and symmetric matrices."},"Rⁿ · 开球":{"d":"The open ball B(p, r) = {x : ‖x−p‖ < r} in Rⁿ is diffeomorphic to Rⁿ itself and is contractible to a point. It is the most trivial smooth manifold — the local model is exactly this.","t":"All open balls are diffeomorphic to one another, yet they differ from the sphere Sⁿ: Sⁿ is not contractible, and the difference lies in the boundary."},"球面 S² / 环面 T²":{"d":"Sphere S²: χ = 2, simply connected; torus T² = R²/Z²: χ = 0, fundamental group Z². Both are compact boundaryless smooth surfaces, but their topology is entirely different.","t":"Classification of closed surfaces: every such surface is homeomorphic to the sphere of some genus g (orientable) or to the connected sum of k projective planes (non-orientable). The sphere is g=0 and the torus is g=1."},"光滑椭圆曲线":{"d":"A smooth (nonsingular) elliptic curve is, as a real surface, a torus: E(C) ≅ C/Λ ≅ T². Its singularities have been resolved, which is why it sits in C1 rather than C2.","t":"As a real manifold it is always a torus, but as a complex manifold there are infinitely many of them — the modulus τ distinguishes them. This is one topology with different complex structures."},"曼德勃罗集边界":{"d":"The boundary ∂M of M is a fractal: its Hausdorff dimension is 2 (Shishikura 1991), yet it is not any curve — it almost fills up the interior.","t":"The dimension of ∂M equals 2, and M itself is also 2-dimensional — the boundary is as large as the interior. This is the most extreme example of a fractal boundary."},"莫比乌斯带 / Hopf 纤维化":{"d":"Möbius band: the nontrivial twist of an interval bundle over S¹; non-orientable, with boundary a single circle. Hopf fibration: S³ → S² with fibre S¹, the first nontrivial fibre bundle.","t":"Both are locally trivial but globally nontrivial: the twist of the Möbius band is w₁ ≠ 0, and the nontriviality of the Hopf fibration is measured by π₃(S²) = Z."},"循环群 C₃":{"d":"C₃ = {e, r, r²}, r³ = e. It is the cyclic group of order 3 and the only group of order 3 (a group of prime order is necessarily cyclic).","t":"Lagrange's theorem: the order of a subgroup divides the order of the group. Since 3 is prime, C₃ has no proper subgroup."},"循环群 C₄":{"d":"C₄ = {e, r, r², r³}, r⁴ = e. It has the proper subgroup {e, r²} ≅ C₂.","t":"C₄ and V₄ both have order 4 but are not isomorphic: C₄ has an element of order 4, whereas every non-identity element of V₄ has order 2. Same order, different isomorphism type — this is the first counterexample."},"循环群 C₆":{"d":"C₆ = {e, r, …, r⁵}, r⁶ = e. It has proper subgroups C₂ and C₃, and C₆ ≅ C₂ × C₃.","t":"The group-theoretic form of the Chinese remainder theorem: C_m × C_n ≅ C_{mn} if and only if gcd(m,n)=1."},"二面体群 D₃":{"d":"D₃ is the full symmetry group of the equilateral triangle: 3 rotations + 3 reflections, 6 elements in all. It is isomorphic to S₃ — every non-abelian group of order 6 is S₃.","t":"D₃ ≅ S₃ is the smallest non-abelian group. Its 3 reflections all have order 2, and the product of two reflections is a rotation — flipping twice equals rotating once."},"二面体群 D₄":{"d":"D₄ is the full symmetry group of the square: 4 rotations + 4 reflections, 8 elements in all. It is at the same time a subgroup of the rotation group of the regular octahedron (S₄).","t":"D₄ and the quaternion group Q₈ are both non-abelian of order 8 but are not isomorphic: D₄ has 5 elements of order 2, Q₈ only 1."},"二面体群 D₆":{"d":"D₆ is the full symmetry group of the regular hexagon: 6 rotations + 6 reflections, 12 elements in all. It is exactly the symmetry group of the snowflake.","t":"D₆ and A₄ both have order 12 but are not isomorphic: D₆ has a normal subgroup C₆ of order 2, A₄ has none."},"Klein 四元群 V₄":{"d":"V₄ = {e, a, b, c}, every non-identity element has order 2, and V₄ ≅ C₂ × C₂. It is the symmetry group of the rectangle (width and height unequal, so a rotation by 90° is not a symmetry).","t":"V₄ is the smallest non-cyclic group. It is a normal subgroup of A₄ and is also part of a Sylow 2-subgroup of S₄."},"正四面体群 A₄":{"d":"A₄ is the group of even permutations of 4 letters, and also the rotation group of the regular tetrahedron: 8 rotations by 120° (about a vertex and the opposite face) + 3 rotations by 180° (about the midpoints of opposite edges) + 1 identity.","t":"A₄ is the key to the smallest counterexample among non-abelian simple groups: it has the normal subgroup V₄ and so is not simple. The smallest non-abelian simple group is A₅ (of order 60)."},"立方体旋转群 S₄":{"d":"The 24 rotations of the cube form a group isomorphic to S₄ — because a rotation is determined completely by how it permutes the four space diagonals. It is at the same time the rotation group of the regular octahedron.","t":"Why 24: the cube has 6 faces; choose one to face up (6 ways), then rotate about the vertical axis (4 ways), 6×4 = 24."},"D₃ 的 Cayley 表":{"d":"The Cayley table lists once and for all what any two elements multiply to: the entry in row i and column j is gᵢgⱼ. Every row and every column contains each element exactly once — this is called a Latin square.","t":"The group table is symmetric ⇔ the group is abelian. The table of D₃ is not symmetric, and this is the direct evidence that it is non-abelian."},"单位根乘群 μ₆":{"d":"μ₆ = {ζ⁰, ζ¹, …, ζ⁵}, ζ = e^{2πi/6}. Multiplication corresponds to addition of angles, so μ₆ ≅ C₆.","t":"The same C₆ has two descriptions: written multiplicatively it is the group of roots of unity, written geometrically it is rotations. The core of abstract group theory is exactly this — regardless of what the elements are, look only at the structure of the operation."},"计数与测量":{"d":"Counting classifies finite sets up to equipotence and assigns them cardinalities; measurement assigns real numbers to the lengths of segments, to areas and to volumes. The two correspond respectively to the primitive lattice points d = 0 and d = 1, n = 1 and n = 2: the natural numbers and the rational numbers arise here, and commensurability is the earliest equivalence relation.","t":"Book V of Euclid's Elements treats measurement through commensurable and incommensurable magnitudes; only with the Dedekind cut in the 19th century was measurement made rigorous as the completeness of the real number system."},"欧几里得几何":{"d":"Euclidean geometry is the geometry of the plane and of space developed from five postulates and five axioms, with straightedge and compass construction as its means of construction. Its objects are points, lines and planes; congruence is characterized by superposition, and the fifth postulate (the parallel postulate) separates it from non-Euclidean geometry.","t":"Proposition 47 of Book I of the Elements gives the Pythagorean theorem, and its proof uses only the first four postulates; the independence of the fifth postulate was established only in the 19th century, through the models of Lobachevsky and Bolyai."},"笛卡尔坐标系":{"d":"A Cartesian coordinate system is the affine isomorphism obtained by choosing an origin and a basis in an affine space and sending a point P to the tuple of coordinates (x₁, …, xₙ). Under this correspondence a curve becomes the solution set of an equation F(x) = 0, geometric objects and algebraic equations translate into one another, and the contravariant correspondence between coordinate rings and point sets holds.","t":"Under this correspondence a line corresponds to a first-degree equation and a conic section to a second-degree equation; algebraic operations and geometric constructions are dual to each other, and the whole method of analytic geometry rests on this one isomorphism."},"牛顿–莱布尼茨微积分":{"d":"Calculus takes the limit as its sole foundation: the derivative is defined as the limit of a difference quotient f′(x) = lim_{h→0}(f(x+h) − f(x))/h, and the definite integral as the limit of Riemann sums as the partition is refined, ∫_a^b f = lim Σ f(ξᵢ)Δxᵢ. The fundamental theorem of calculus links the two as mutually inverse operations.","t":"Fundamental theorem of calculus: if f is continuous on [a,b], then F(x) = ∫_a^x f is differentiable with F′ = f, hence ∫_a^b f = F(b) − F(a)."},"伽罗瓦理论":{"d":"Galois theory takes as its object the automorphism group of a field extension: for a finite Galois extension L/K, Gal(L/K) is the group of L-automorphisms fixing K, and its order equals the degree [L:K] of the extension. Solvability of a polynomial by radicals is thereby translated into solvability of a group over its splitting field.","t":"Galois correspondence: intermediate fields E and subgroups H of Gal(L/K) correspond one-to-one in reverse order, and E/K is normal if and only if H is normal; f is solvable by radicals if and only if Gal(f) is solvable."},"黎曼几何":{"d":"Riemannian geometry is the geometry obtained by giving a smooth manifold M a Riemannian metric g: g assigns to each point a positive definite inner product on the tangent space, varying smoothly with the point. From g alone are determined the Levi-Civita connection, the geodesics, the Riemann curvature tensor and the volume measure; curvature measures the deviation from Euclidean space.","t":"The Riemann curvature tensor vanishes identically at a point if and only if the metric is isometric to the Euclidean metric on a neighbourhood of that point; a complete space of constant sectional curvature K is of one of only three kinds — Euclidean, spherical, hyperbolic."},"广义相对论":{"d":"General relativity models spacetime as a four-dimensional pseudo-Riemannian manifold (M, g), with g of Lorentzian signature (−, +, +, +). The distribution of matter is described by the energy-momentum tensor T; gravity is not a force but the curvature of the metric, and the field equation is Ric − ½Rg + Λg = 8πT.","t":"The spherically symmetric solution of the vacuum field equation R_{μν} = 0 is unique, namely the Schwarzschild solution (Birkhoff's theorem); r = 2M is a coordinate singularity, and the curvature invariants are finite there."},"格罗滕迪克概型":{"d":"A scheme is a locally ringed space locally isomorphic to some Spec R; morphisms are morphisms of locally ringed spaces, and all of them together form the category Sch. It generalizes the points of a variety to prime ideals, so that nilpotent elements, equations over non-algebraically-closed fields and fibre products can all be discussed; geometric points are characterized by morphisms with values in an arbitrary field.","t":"In the category of schemes fibre products and base change always exist, and the geometric properties of morphisms (flat, smooth, proper) are preserved under base change, which makes it possible to treat a family of parameters uniformly."},"芒德勃罗分形":{"d":"A fractal is a set whose Hausdorff dimension is strictly greater than its topological dimension; a typical source is a self-similar compact set satisfying K = ∪ f_i(K), with f_i a similarity of ratio r_i, whose dimension s is determined by Moran's equation Σ r_i^s = 1.","t":"If a self-similar set satisfies the open set condition, its Hausdorff dimension equals the similarity dimension s, and 0 < H^s(K) < ∞; the boundary of the Mandelbrot set has dimension 2."},"弦论与几何":{"d":"String theory replaces the point by a two-dimensional worldsheet Σ embedded in ten-dimensional spacetime as the fundamental object; its action is the Polyakov action, and quantization requires the background metric to satisfy a first-order correction of the Einstein equation. To agree with four-dimensional observation, the extra six dimensions are compactified on a Calabi–Yau threefold.","t":"The Hodge numbers (h^{1,1}, h^{2,1}) of a Calabi–Yau threefold determine the low-energy particle spectrum; mirror symmetry asserts that two families with interchanged Hodge numbers give the same theory."}};
